STEP 4 -- the refutation that was attempted and failed to fire.

  If this table were an anti-debug kit rather than Live Coding, the debug
  information classes would be in the same neighbourhood. Searched in the
  LiveCoding module source:

  $ grep -rn "ProcessDebugPort\|ProcessDebugFlags\|ThreadHideFromDebugger\|SystemKernelDebugger" \
        --include=*.h --include=*.cpp Engine/Source/Developer/Windows/LiveCoding
  (no output)

  The NtQueryInformationProcess call sites in LC_Process.cpp pass
  ProcessBasicInformation (lines 513, 667) and ProcessWow64Information
  (lines 372, 528). Neither is a debug class.

  Searched in the Shipping binary itself: ProcessDebugPort, ProcessDebugFlags,
  ProcessDebugObjectHandle, ThreadHideFromDebugger,
  SystemKernelDebuggerInformation, CONTEXT_DEBUG_REGISTERS, NtGlobalFlag,
  BeingDebugged -- ABSENT, all of them, both encodings.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE ON THE LIMITS OF STEPS 2 AND 3

The source tree is an oracle of class `filesystem` plus `external-doc`: it proves
what the Unreal Engine 5.4 source on THIS machine contains, not what was
compiled into this binary. The conclusion "this table belongs to Live Coding" is
therefore INFERRED, not OBSERVED, and it is a statement about the D-04 oracle.
Decision D-04 requires every conclusion drawn on that file to be re-verified on
the Shipping binary before it counts. Here the re-verification is the STEP 1
result: none of the ten strings exists in the Shipping binary at all.
